<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Vänern</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vänern</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Vänern is the largest lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, covering about 5,650 square kilometres in the west of the country. It drains through the Göta älv river to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a>. The lands around its shores, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vastergotland">Västergötland</a> and Värmland, were part of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geats">Geats</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-vanern.jpg" alt="Lake Vänern" width="320" height="112" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Achird · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H%C3%A4llekis_industrier_sedda_fr_utsiktstornet_p%C3%A5_Kinnekulle_6116.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
